      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Cardiovascular disease, especially ischemic heart disease, is a major comorbidity in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) patients. Several studies suggested that after acute exacerbation of COPD (AECOPD), there is a significant increase of mortality (cardiac and all-cause) and of myocardial infarction. Whether cardiac troponin (Tn) elevation during AECOPD could be considered a prognostic marker of all-cause mortality is still debated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "To assess the prognostic role of cardiac Tn elevation during AECOPD, we performed a systematic review and meta-analysis. We included studies with patients admitted to the hospital for AECOPD, with at least one Tn assessment and reporting the relationship (after multivariable analysis) between Tn elevation and all-cause mortality. Secondarily, studies were stratified according to: i) type of troponin (Tn I or Tn T), and ii) follow-up length (≤6 months vs. >6 months)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Ten studies were included in the systematic review and 8 in the meta-analysis. Cardiac Tn elevation ranges from 18% to 73%. We found that cardiac Tn elevation was significantly related to an increased risk for all-cause mortality (OR 1.69; 95% CI 1.25-2.29; I(2) 40%). This finding was independent to the follow-up length of studies (≤6 months: OR 3.22; 95% CI 1.31-7.91; >6 months: OR 1.38; 95% CI 1.02-1.86). Finally, Tn T seems to be more helpful in predicting all-cause mortality as compared to Tn I (OR 1.54; 95% CI 1.2-1.96 vs. OR 3.39, 95% CI 0.86-13.36, respectively)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In patients admitted to the hospital for AECOPD, cardiac Tn elevation emerged as an independent predictor of increased risk of all-cause mortality."
          }
        ]
      },
